# Snapback 2 — agent command sheet

Read this, then `rules/RULES.md` and `rules/NOT-DOING.md` at the repo root, then
`llp/1000-snapback2.explainer.md`; the specs are LLP 1001 (engine), 1002 (loop), 1003 (effects), 1012 (local-first v1; Review, binds after the loop bar).

```
cargo build                          # data plane + binary; no JS engine, no bootstrap
rust_test_threads="${SNAPBACK_RUST_TEST_THREADS:-$(node -e 'process.stdout.write(String(Math.max(8, Math.min(32, require("node:os").availableParallelism()))))' 2>/dev/null || printf '8\n')}"
NEXTEST_TEST_THREADS="$rust_test_threads" cargo nextest run \
  --config-file ../.config/nextest.toml --profile local-proof \
  --workspace --no-fail-fast          # Rust executable tests (~36 s on the measured EPYC host)
cargo test --workspace --doc --no-fail-fast  # workspace doctests omitted by nextest
scripts/test.sh                      # everything blocking (~84 s warm on EPYC; 60 s total budget)
node scripts/measure.mjs --check     # loop numbers → metrics/loop.jsonl; fails on regression
node scripts/publish.mjs --dry-run   # production build WITH the engine + npm pack; drop --dry-run to ship
scripts/hermes test -p snapback2-effects   # the engine proof (Ibex 2 + vanilla Hermes); never blocking
(cd packages/snapback2 && node build.mjs)  # bundle the compiler worker, client, react, test runner
./target/debug/snapback2 doctor      # boot-to-ready proof: certifies programs, finds the compiler worker
```

Using the product from a project (the shim sets `SNAPBACK2_COMPILER`):
`snapback2 init [dir] [--template todos|chat] [--client react|expo|none] [--json]` (Expo requires chat; Quarry `snapback/*.q`),
`snapback2 dev` (prints `SNAPBACK_URL=…`, detaches, attaches if running, `--stop`/`--status`),
`snapback2 check`, `run <op> [args] --as alice|system`, `test`, `why [--as system]`, `trace latest`, `data [--as system]`, `logs`,
`persona list`, `reset`, `guide`.
Jobs: `run <job> [args] --as system [--plan|--wait|--dry-run]`, `run --file repair.q [args] --as system [--plan|--wait|--dry-run]`
(`--dry-run` rehearses on a discarded snapshot: no effects, timers, sweeps, or blob writes),
`run --cancel|--resume <job_id> --as system`, `trace <job_id>`. See `guide job`.

Gotchas you cannot derive from the code:
- Own Cargo and Bun workspaces; the repo root excludes them. Run cargo/bun from `snapback2/`.
- LLPs for Snapback 2 are numbered **1000+** in the shared `llp/`; `llp-create` must be told.
- No file over 1,500 lines — `caps` (root `node scripts/caps.mjs`) refuses it. Split on the way in.
- The corpus under `corpus/` is shared by the Rust engine, the TS evaluator, and the TS compiler;
  regenerate with `cargo run -p snapback2-core --bin export_corpus`.
- `effects` is off by default; `hermes` needs `IBEX2_VANILLA_HERMES_DIR` + `IBEX2_HERMESC` (see
  `scripts/hermes`). The blocking checks never depend on it; the published binary carries it.
- Backends use `snapback/schema.q`; TypeScript under `snapback/` is reserved for effects, seeds, and journeys.
- The binary needs the bundled compiler worker (`packages/snapback2/dist/compiler.mjs`) for any verb
  that compiles; run `node build.mjs` in `packages/snapback2` after touching the compiler.
- `metrics/loop.jsonl` is append-only and conflicts on every merge; resolve by union.
- Reuse a named lane worktree; never `git stash`; push after `scripts/test.sh` is green.
# Rules

**Snapback 2 optimizes the edit-to-verified loop — the agent's building an app on it, and ours building it.**

Speed is the constraint. Everything below exists to keep the edit-to-verified loop short
and to stop this repository accreting apparatus faster than anyone can remove it. These
rules bind `snapback2/` and the LLPs numbered 1000 and up; Snapback 1 is baselined, not
exempt, and its numbers may only fall.

Each rule is **[check]** (enforced by `caps`) or **[review]** (enforced by a human).

## Budgets — every budget is a trade, not a limit

- **5 blocking checks, 60s total.** A sixth requires deleting one, in the same PR. **[check]**
- **59 documents in the working set** (`llp/current/`). Today's count. A 60th requires
  archiving one — which is just removing the link. Snapback 2 aims to leave this at 15;
  Snapback 1's links are archived at promotion. **[check]**
- **12 documents in the foundation** (`llp/foundation/`). **[check]**
- **This file: 700 words.** If it grows, something becomes a check or stops being a rule. **[check]**
- **1,500 lines per source file, baseline 204.** Generated files exempt — and generated
  files are built, not committed; so are benchmark run records under `benchmarks/runs/`,
  which are evidence, not source. The baseline is Snapback 1's oversize count on the day
  the rule landed; it may not grow, and `snapback2/` contributes zero to it. **[check]**

## Blocking checks

`caps` · `ref-check` · `snapback2/scripts/test.sh` · `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs --check` · `check-inventories`

## Loop shape

- **Every check reports all failures in one run.** Fail-fast is banned. **[check]**
- **Nothing blocks on anything slower than 60 seconds.** Slow verification runs on a
  schedule, attributed to the breaking commit, and files a bounded set of issues. **[review]**
- **Fix loops get 3 rounds.** Then stop: escalate to a human or descope. **[review]**

## Time budgets

Tracked every run of `snapback2/scripts/measure.mjs` (appends `snapback2/metrics/loop.jsonl`).
A regression is a P0 with a name on it.

| | budget |
|---|---|
| Incremental rebuild after a one-line edit | 15s |
| Test what you just changed | 30s |
| Blocking gate suite | 60s |
| Full build, warm cache | 5 min |
| **`snapback2 check` on a program (the agent's product latency)** | **1s** |
| **`snapback2 doctor` boot-to-ready** | **0.5s** |

## Scope

- **`NOT-DOING.md` is binding.** Moving something onto the doing-list means writing why
  and taking something off. **[review]**
- **Delete; don't deprecate.** No compat shims or migration paths before promotion. **[review]**
- **Nothing is compiled, transpiled, or generated at runtime that could be built ahead of
  time.** **[review]**
- **A spec needs an implementer and a date, or it isn't written.** **[review]**
- **Only implemented specs bind.** A document with no running code behind it is a
  proposal, and a proposal cannot block a PR or justify a check. **[review]**

## Agents

- **Agents remove apparatus freely and add none.** An agent PR that adds a check, script,
  registry, config file, or design doc needs explicit human approval saying so. **[review]**
- **2026-09-06 session exception:** the user delegated the search implementation
  decisions; one `snapback2-core/build.rs` may generate pinned Unicode tables at
  build time, alongside the existing package build. No new gate, registry,
  runtime generator, or Unicode dependency is authorized by this exception. **[review]**
- **Snapback 2 LLPs are numbered 1000 and up**; `llp-create` must be told. **[review]**

## Shared machines

- Warm, named lanes, one per stream of work. Never clone fresh; reuse the worktree.
- **Never `git stash`.** Use a temporary commit.
- Kill only PIDs you recorded at launch. Never by name or pattern.
# What Snapback 2 Does Not Do

This is the most valuable file in the repository. It is longer than the doing-list on
purpose. Moving something off it means writing one line naming what it unblocks and
taking something off the doing-list in the same change.

## The bars that make this list derivable

Three bars, named separately so one name never covers two objects (LLP 1007 §4a;
the author delegated the first two to the orchestrator on 2026-09-02 and added
the third on 2026-09-04, LLP 1012 §12):

- **The loop bar.** A fresh agent, with only the installed package and the task,
  builds the reduced Clique M1–M3 (accounts, feed, realtime chat; React web; no
  media) on the **published** snapback2 in under 1,200 s with at most one
  Snapback-attributed correction loop, and `snapback2 test` proves it
  (LLP 0329.016 §8). Everything not required by this bar does not exist yet.
- **The capability bar.** `benchmarks/briefs/social-messenger.md` M1–M3 as
  written: Expo on iOS, Android, and web; an avatar; one to four images and one
  video; no external I/O. It starts only after the loop bar is met on a
  published package. When it starts, LLP 1006's first slice is the brief's
  formats **plus HEIC and QuickTime MOV** (author ruling 2026-09-03, held over
  a 2–1 panel recommendation): iPhone cameras produce both, and the brief's
  own real-phone acceptance cannot pass without them. The offsetting item is
  non-media `file` columns, which LLP 1006 §13a designs and defers to a later
  slice. Until then the media and Expo slice stays on this list.
- **The offline bar.** `benchmarks/briefs/social-messenger.md` M5 acceptance 5,
  M6 acceptance 1 and 2, LLP 1012 §10's physical process-kill witness, and
  one acceptance only the client twin can pass (a never-opened chat renders
  offline through its query; an offline send is predicted into that thread),
  green on the Expo iOS simulator and React web on a **published** package
  (author ruling 2026-09-04, LLP 1012 §12; the twin acceptance from the
  author's principle the same day: go straight to the ideal, no staging). It starts when the loop bar is met
  and runs alongside the capability bar's Expo arm, which shares the carrier.
  Until then local-first v1 is specified, not built.

## Not building

- **A second authoring profile, a plan algebra, or a JS handler lane.** One language —
  Quarry (LLP 1004; the TypeScript authoring surface was deleted on 2026-09-02, LLP
  1007 §4b) — one spelling. Quarry binds directly to typed IR. TypeScript is limited to
  seeds, journeys, and effect bodies, never a lowering target or a way to author tables,
  rules, queries, or mutations. Familiar vs Explicit cost every v1 doc twice (LLP 0317
  bundle 6).
- **JavaScript in the data plane.** Queries and mutations run in the Rust IR
  interpreter inside the SQLite transaction. Ibex 2 is linked only by
  `snapback2-effects`, only with `--features hermes`, only for effects.
- **Committed generated code.** Types by inference; IR, certificate, and stubs are a
  gitignored build cache. No `generated/`, no review manifest, no digest-named imports.
- **An auth *mode*.** Authentication is always on; development has personas (`--as`).
- **A supervised bundler.** The server never spawns, proxies, or watches Metro or Vite.
  The client contract is `SNAPBACK_URL`.
- **Exact 2 until Unicode prefix search qualifies on React web and Expo**, and
  client arms beyond those three. The 2026-09-06 session-delegated move below
  postpones Exact 2 to fund coherent indexed online/offline search. No Swift,
  Kotlin, or Dart.
- **Media collections, push, cron, vector/ranked search, hosted control planes,
  multi-region, federation.** Exact Unicode word-prefix indexing is the narrow
  2026-09-06 search exception below. Assets (typed intent), one `channel` kind, one `schedule` kind, one
  hosted target: that is the feature list until the bar is met. Exception (author ruling 2026-09-05, LLP 1015 §7, §8.7): lane H0 of the
  control plane — `deploy` to a directory, the release ledger, snapshots, `rollback`,
  `secret`, and token admission in `serve` mode; no provider, no registry — may start
  before the loop bar. The hosted lanes H1–H3 still wait for it and for LLP 1015 §11.
- **Offline beyond local-first v1** (LLP 1012, author rulings 2026-09-04). v1 is the
  result cache over a durable per-principal range store, `until-revoked` and
  `delivered-history` retention, digest-checked re-authorization at reconnect, a
  FIFO outbox settled by OCC, local evaluation by the twin, and asset bytes under
  the owning row's lease. Not built until a brief's number asks (LLP 1012 §11):
  hold sets, the dependency-aware outbox, per-row delta resume with tombstones,
  per-field `unheld`, a store budget with eviction priority,
  cancel/`asOf`, `purge-on-block`, `all-owned`, `merge` modes and `sequence`, any
  at-rest encryption claim, and device-to-device sync.
- **Observability and analytics beyond `why`, `trace`, `logs`, and `data`** (LLP
  1009, shelved 2026-09-04). Slid off the doing-list to pay for local-first v1
  (LLP 1012 §13); returns when the offline bar is met.
- **A guide.** `snapback2 guide` serves the compiler's facts, a one-screen grammar, and
  about twelve recipes with CI-executed snippets. No million-word corpus, no embedded
  20 MB guide in the binary, no calendar freshness dates.
- **WASM anywhere.** The client evaluator is the TypeScript twin. The 2026-09-06
  search trade, revised under the 2026-09-07 architecture and client-item commissions, sets
  **84,000 bytes gzip for the complete local runtime**, with **80,000 the working
  target**. The canonical measurement uses Node 24.18.0, esbuild 0.25.9, gzip level 9.
  All normalization data, decoding, indexes, incremental state, prediction and
  local cursors participate in the measured entry; relocating required bytes
  does not count as a saving. Historical measurements remain in
  `snapback2/metrics/twin.jsonl`; the complete search integration is not yet
  qualified. The measured scope revision in LLP1012 records why the earlier
  34,000, 50,000 and 66,000 allocations were replaced; the item-surface trade in LLP 1018.003 records the 84,000 amendment; exceeding 84,000 requires
  a new explicit scope decision.

## Process not doing

- **No drift registries, inventories, line-pinned diagnostics registries, claim
  digests, or frozen-record gates.** They were 188 open / 623 closed v1 tickets by
  themselves (LLP 0329.016 §2.2 M8). Generated projections are built, not committed.
- **No gate bound to an exact commit; no push gated on a battery.** Five blocking
  checks, sixty seconds, run locally before push. Everything slower runs on a schedule
  and files a bounded set of issues.
- **No fresh worktrees per task.** Named lanes, reused, warm.
- **No `snapback2 <verb>` added without its row in LLP 0329.016 §5.11** and its entry in
  the measured loop.
- **No diagnostic code without a family, a site, a rewrite, and a page.** ~20 families,
  not 3,597 codes.
- **No spec without an implementer and a date; only implemented specs bind.** LLP
  0329.014.004 and 0329.016 are proposals until the code they describe runs here.
- **No files over 1,500 lines**, including ones we would like to steal from v1. Split
  them on the way in or leave them.

## Deliberately worse

- No backwards compatibility with Snapback 1 apps, packages, or on-disk formats.
- No API stability before promotion; every publish is `0.0.x`.
- Sparse prose documentation; the diagnostics and the recipes carry the load.
- One hosted target, one storage engine (SQLite), one platform binary at a time.

## Moving something off this list

Write one line naming what it unblocks, and take something off the doing-list in the same
PR. If nothing can come off, the answer is no.

